  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 306 PM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nSurface high pressure is building into the region today and a cooler \nand much drier air mass is now in place. Temperatures have risen \ninto the low-mid 80s and dew points have dropped into the upper 50s \nin a few places. Efficient boundary layer mixing has allowed dew \npoints to fall a bit below guidance. We're expecting similar mixing \nconditions on Thursday so will nudge dew points downward. \nAdditionally, high temperatures may run a few degrees above guidance \nas well. Still, near to slightly below-average readings are \nexpected.\n\nThe next few nights may see some patchy fog, which may be locally \ndense at times. Fog is not expected to be widespread, confined \nmainly to rural areas and near/along rivers. Fog may linger into the \nmorning hours before rapidly dissipating as mixing resumes.\n\nAloft, troughing over the northeastern US will promote northwesterly \nflow across the Midwest. Ensemble guidance is in good agreement \nshowing a shortwave trough diving southeast within this flow, \narriving late Friday. A round of showers and thunderstorms is \npossible late Friday evening and overnight. Guidance then slows the \nshortwave down as western ridging amplifies. This should bring high \nrain probabilities into the day Saturday, potentially into early \nSunday. Locally heavy rainfall amounts are possible owing to the \nslow system motion.\n\nLooking into next week, the weekend shortwave departs eastward while \nthe polar jet lifts north. Though there is no strong signal for \neither troughing or ridging, the jet stream residing well to our \nnorth should allow temperatures to creep upwards. A warming trend is \ntherefore favored next week with highs potentially reaching into the \nupper 80s / near 90 at times. Some guidance keeps the jet stream \nactive, with frequent storm systems embedded within the broader \nflow. This may act to keep daily precipitation chances above average \nthrough next week as well. However, the signal regarding \nprecipitation is less clear than for temperatures.",
